{"id":"q12","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"puzzle","question":"A farmer must cross a river with a goat. His boat holds the farmer and one animal. Does he need more than one crossing to get himself and the goat to the other side?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"A single crossing is enough for the farmer and goat.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Classic river-crossing puzzles often involve multiple passengers and constraints that force multiple trips.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The wording 'more than one crossing' might suggest a trap like in classic puzzles.","confidence":0.3,"lean":1,"theta":0.4712,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The boat's limited capacity might lead a reader to think the farmer must ferry the goat separately.","confidence":0.2,"lean":1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The boat holds the farmer plus one animal, and the goat is that one animal, so both can ride together.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"No other item needs to be transported.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"No safety constraint prevents the farmer and goat from crossing together.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The boat's capacity exactly fits the farmer and goat, so one trip suffices.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":2,"j":7,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":3,"